Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 05:07:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Input: atmel_mxt_ts - optionally enable T97 on start

With the info block checksum read fixed, the maXTouch-compatible
controller on a Xiaomi Mi 4i (ferrari) replacement panel probes, but
reports no T100 touches. The Xiaomi vendor driver writes ENABLE | RPTEN
to the CTRL byte of T97 (PTC Key Set) instances 0 to 2 each time it
resumes the controller, and clears them on suspend.

The vendor driver's resume sequence (T97 CTRL, T19, T7 restore,
CALIBRATE) was bisected on the device, with a power cycle before each
case, a person touching the panel continuously and interrupts counted.
Writing ENABLE | RPTEN to T97 instance 0 alone makes the controller
report touches; the T7 deep sleep/run cycle, CALIBRATE and the T19 write
make no difference. The setting is lost on reset, and a maxtouch.cfg
cannot be used to make it stick: the controller never reports the
checksum of an uploaded configuration, so the driver would upload the
file and issue BACKUPNV on every boot.

When the "atmel,enable-t97" property is set and the device has a T97
object, write CTRL = ENABLE | RPTEN to T97 instance 0 from mxt_start():
in deep sleep mode before restoring T7 and recalibrating (the vendor
order), and in T9 control mode after the soft reset, which would
otherwise undo it. The byte is written without a read-modify-write
because the controller's registers cannot be read back reliably.
Devices without the property are unaffected.

If the controller resets itself while the input device is open, T97
stays disabled until the next mxt_start(). This was not seen in testing.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_mxt_ts.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_mxt_ts.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_mxt_ts.c
index 2c0e5a6713..5bdf84d97a 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_mxt_ts.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_mxt_ts.c
@@ -159,6 +159,11 @@ struct t37_debug {
 #define MXT_RESET_VALUE		0x01
 #define MXT_BACKUP_VALUE	0x55
 
+/* T97 PTC Key Set */
+#define MXT_T97_CTRL		0
+#define MXT_T97_CTRL_ENABLE	BIT(0)
+#define MXT_T97_CTRL_RPTEN	BIT(1)
+
 /* T100 Multiple Touch Touchscreen */
 #define MXT_T100_CTRL		0
 #define MXT_T100_CFG1		1
@@ -355,6 +360,8 @@ struct mxt_data {
 
 	enum mxt_suspend_mode suspend_mode;
 
+	bool enable_t97;
+
 	u32 wakeup_method;
 
 	struct touchscreen_properties prop;
@@ -3062,6 +3069,25 @@ static struct attribute *mxt_attrs[] = {
 
 ATTRIBUTE_GROUPS(mxt);
 
+/*
+ * The maXTouch-compatible controller on some Xiaomi Mi 4i (ferrari) panels
+ * does not report any touches until T97 instance 0 is enabled, and loses
+ * that setting on reset. Its registers cannot be read back reliably, so the
+ * control byte is written without a read-modify-write.
+ */
+static void mxt_enable_t97(struct mxt_data *data)
+{
+	int error;
+
+	if (!data->enable_t97 || !data->T97_reportid_min)
+		return;
+
+	error = mxt_write_object(data, MXT_TOUCH_PTC_KEYS_T97, MXT_T97_CTRL,
+				 MXT_T97_CTRL_ENABLE | MXT_T97_CTRL_RPTEN);
+	if (error)
+		dev_warn(&data->client->dev, "Failed to enable T97: %d\n", error);
+}
+
 static void mxt_start(struct mxt_data *data)
 {
 	mxt_wakeup_toggle(data->client, true, false);
@@ -3069,6 +3095,7 @@ static void mxt_start(struct mxt_data *data)
 	switch (data->suspend_mode) {
 	case MXT_SUSPEND_T9_CTRL:
 		mxt_soft_reset(data);
+		mxt_enable_t97(data);
 
 		/* Touch enable */
 		/* 0x83 = SCANEN | RPTEN | ENABLE */
@@ -3078,6 +3105,7 @@ static void mxt_start(struct mxt_data *data)
 
 	case MXT_SUSPEND_DEEP_SLEEP:
 	default:
+		mxt_enable_t97(data);
 		mxt_set_t7_power_cfg(data, MXT_POWER_CFG_RUN);
 
 		/* Recalibrate since chip has been in deep sleep */
@@ -3182,6 +3210,8 @@ static int mxt_parse_device_properties(struct mxt_data *data)
 		data->t15_num_keys = n_keys;
 	}
 
+	data->enable_t97 = device_property_read_bool(dev, "atmel,enable-t97");
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
